**1. Introduction**

This document outlines the technical specifications for the logistics, warehousing, inventory management, supplier coordination, and distribution network processes for Global Supply Chain Solutions, Inc. (GSCS). The aim is to optimize the supply chain operations by detailing requirements, constraints, and key relationships between entities involved in the logistics process.

---

**2. Scope**

The scope of this specification includes:

- Overview of logistics and warehousing operations
- Inventory management requirements
- Supplier and vendor relationships
- Distribution network setup and optimization
- Key performance indicators (KPIs)

---

**3. Logistics Operations Overview**

Global Supply Chain Solutions operates from three major distribution centers located in:

- **Dallas, Texas** (DC#1)
- **Chicago, Illinois** (DC#2)
- **Los Angeles, California** (DC#3)

Each distribution center utilizes the **SAP Extended Warehouse Management (EWM)** system for real-time inventory tracking and management.

**3.1 Key Personnel**

- **Emily Johnson** – Logistics Coordinator (GSCS)
- **Michael Smith** – Inventory Manager (DC#1)
- **Sarah Lee** – Supplier Relations Specialist (GSCS)
- **James Brown** – Distribution Network Analyst (GSCS)

**3.2 Logistics Flow**

Goods are sourced from various suppliers and transported to the distribution centers. The logistics flow involves:

1. Supplier Procurement
2. Transportation to Distribution Centers
3. Warehousing and Inventory Management
4. Order Fulfillment
5. Distribution to Retail and Wholesale Customers

---

**4. Warehousing Requirements**

**4.1 Warehouse Layout**

Each distribution center must be structured to maximize efficiency. The following design specifications are required:

- **Storage Capacity**: Each DC should handle a minimum of 50,000 SKUs (Stock Keeping Units) with a maximum weight capacity of 1 million pounds.
- **Receiving Area**: A minimum of 10,000 square feet for receiving and inspection of incoming goods.
- **Picking Area**: A dedicated 20,000 square feet for order picking operations.
- **Packing Area**: At least 5,000 square feet for packing and preparing orders for shipment.

**4.2 Technology Integration**

The integration of **RFID (Radio Frequency Identification)** technologies for inventory tracking is mandatory. Each SKU must be tagged with an RFID chip to:

- Reduce errors in inventory counts
- Improve the speed of item retrieval
- Facilitate real-time inventory visibility

---

**5. Inventory Management Requirements**

**5.1 Inventory Control**

The **ABC Inventory Analysis** methodology will be employed to categorize inventory into three classes:

- **Class A**: High-value items (top 20% of inventory, representing 80% of total value)
- **Class B**: Moderate-value items (next 30%)
- **Class C**: Low-value items (remaining 50%)

**5.2 Inventory Audits**

Regular audits must be conducted quarterly. The inventory management team, led by Michael Smith, will ensure that:

- Cycle counts are performed bi-weekly for Class A items
- Class B and C items are counted monthly and quarterly, respectively

**5.3 Safety Stock Levels**

Safety stock levels are to be maintained at 10% of the average monthly usage for Class A items and 5% for Class B items to mitigate risks of stockouts.

---

**6. Supplier and Vendor Relationships**

**6.1 Supplier Selection Criteria**

Suppliers must meet the following criteria to be considered:

- Must have a minimum of five years of experience in logistics and supply chain management
- Proven track record of timely delivery (95% on-time delivery rate)
- Ability to provide real-time tracking information via EDI (Electronic Data Interchange)

**6.2 Key Suppliers**

- **ABC Manufacturing Co.**: Provides electronic components; accounts for 30% of supply volume.
- **XYZ Logistics Inc.**: Primary transportation partner for shipping goods from suppliers to DCs.
- **123 Packaging Solutions**: Supplies packaging materials for order fulfillment.

**6.3 Vendor Management System**

The **Coupa Vendor Management System** will be utilized for managing supplier contracts, performance evaluation, and compliance checks.

---

**7. Distribution Network Optimization**

**7.1 Network Design**

The distribution network will utilize a hub-and-spoke model, where:

- **Hubs**: The three distribution centers act as primary hubs.
- **Spokes**: Retail and wholesale customer locations are the spokes, serviced by direct truck routes.

**7.2 Transportation Management System (TMS)**

The **Oracle Transportation Management (OTM)** system will be implemented to:

- Optimize routing and scheduling of deliveries
- Track shipments in real-time
- Analyze transportation costs and performance metrics

**7.3 Delivery Performance Metrics**

Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) to be monitored include:

- On-time delivery rate: Target of 98%
- Transportation cost per shipment: Must not exceed $150
- Order accuracy rate: Target of 99%

---

**8. Constraints**

- **Regulatory Compliance**: All operations must comply with local, state, and federal regulations, including the **Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA)** standards for warehouse safety.
- **Budget Limitations**: The logistics budget for FY 2024 is capped at $5 million, requiring strategic cost management.
- **Seasonal Demand Variability**: Anticipated spikes in demand during Q4 (October-December) necessitate flexible staffing and inventory strategies.

---

**9. Conclusion**

This technical specification outlines the essential requirements and constraints for the logistics, warehousing, inventory management, supplier coordination, and distribution network processes at Global Supply Chain Solutions, Inc. By adhering to these specifications, GSCS aims to enhance operational efficiency, reduce costs, and improve customer satisfaction in its supply chain operations.

